UI primitives that already exist in `@teable/ui-lib/shadcn`: `dialog`, `input-otp`, `badge`, `switch`, `button`, `input`, `label`, `sonner`. No new dependencies are needed.

### 2.2 The verification-code leak (fixed in this PR)

Two flows put the code inside a JWT and send that JWT to the browser:

- **Signup**: `LocalAuthService.jwtSignupCode(email, code)` signs `{ email, code }`. The token is returned by `POST /api/auth/send-signup-verification-code` and in the 422 body from `POST /api/auth/signup`.
- **Change email**: `LocalAuthService.sendChangeEmailCode` signs `{ email, newEmail, code }`. The token is returned by `POST /api/auth/send-change-email-code`.

A JWT is signed but **not encrypted**, so anyone can base64-decode the payload and read the code.

**Impact:**
- Signup email verification can be bypassed: anyone can register with an address they don't own.
- Change-email ownership proof can be bypassed: a signed-in user (or anyone with a stolen session plus the password) can move the account to an address they don't control.
- The codes are 4 digits (10,000 possibilities) and nothing limits how many guesses one token gets.

**Fix:** codes are stored on the server and only a hash is kept. The client only ever gets an opaque random token (section 4).

## 3. Goals and non-goals

**Goals**

- A user with a password can turn email 2FA on or off from Profile Modal → Account security.
- With 2FA on, password sign-in needs a 6-digit code emailed to the account address before a session is created.
- Turning 2FA on or off requires the password plus an emailed code. A stolen session cookie alone can't disable it.
- All emailed codes (signup, change email, 2FA) are stored server-side, hashed, single-use, expire, and allow at most 5 guesses.

**Non-goals (v1)**

- Challenging social / OIDC logins.
- Authenticator-app TOTP, WebAuthn, recovery codes. Recovery codes add little for email 2FA: password reset uses the same inbox.
- "Trust this device for 30 days".
- Admin reset of a user's 2FA (planned for v2; see section 12).
- Signing out other sessions when 2FA is turned on.
- Personal access tokens, MCP / OAuth-app access tokens. These are token-based, never go through `/auth/signin`, and are not affected.

## 4. Shared component: `VerificationCodeService`

New: `apps/nestjs-backend/src/features/auth/verification-code/verification-code.service.ts`, exported from a small module that `LocalAuthModule` and `TwoFactorModule` import.

### 4.1 API

```ts
export type VerificationPurpose = 'signup' | 'change-email' | '2fa-setup' | '2fa-signin';

interface IVerificationEntry<T> {
  codeHash: string;   // hex HMAC
  attempts: number;
  expiresAt: number;  // epoch ms; used to keep the remaining TTL when re-saving after a wrong guess
  data: T;            // what the code is bound to (email, userId, action, ...)
}

issue<T>(purpose: VerificationPurpose, data: T, expiresIn: string):
  Promise<{ token: string; code: string; expiresTime: string }>;

verify<T>(purpose: VerificationPurpose, token: string, code: string): Promise<T>; // consumes on success

peek<T>(purpose: VerificationPurpose, token: string): Promise<T | undefined>;    // for resend; no attempt counted

revoke(purpose: VerificationPurpose, token: string): Promise<void>;
```

### 4.2 Rules

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| Token | `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`. Opaque, carries no data. |
| Code | 6 digits: `crypto.randomInt(0, 1_000_000).toString().padStart(6, '0')`. |
| Storage | Cache key `verify-code:{purpose}:{token}` → `IVerificationEntry`, with TTL = `expiresIn`. |
| Hash | `HMAC-SHA256(authConfig.jwt.secret, "{purpose}:{token}:{code}")`. The plaintext code is never stored, so a leaked cache can't be brute-forced offline without the secret. |
| Compare | `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. |
| Wrong guess | `attempts += 1`. At 5 the entry is deleted. Otherwise it is re-saved with TTL `expiresAt - now`. Throws `INVALID_CAPTCHA` (`httpErrors.auth.verificationCodeInvalid`). |
| Missing / expired | Throws `INVALID_CAPTCHA` (`httpErrors.auth.verificationCodeExpired`). |
| Success | Entry deleted (single use), returns `data`. |
| Dev | Code is still logged when `baseConfig.enableEmailCodeConsole` is on (existing behaviour in `MailSenderService`). |

Add to `cache/types.ts`:

```ts
[key: `verify-code:${string}:${string}`]: {
  codeHash: string;
  attempts: number;
  expiresAt: number;
  data: Record<string, unknown>;
};
```

Constant: `MAX_VERIFICATION_ATTEMPTS = 5`.

### 4.3 Moving signup and change-email onto it (the leak fix)

The wire contract stays the same: the client still gets `{ token, expiresTime }` / `{ token }` and sends `{ token, code }` back. **No frontend changes are needed for the fix.** The only visible change is that codes are 6 digits instead of 4. Both inputs are free text, so this needs no UI change.

| Flow | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| `sendSignupVerificationCode(email)` | `jwtSignupCode(email, code)` | `issue('signup', { email }, authConfig.signupVerificationExpiresIn)` |
| `verifySignup(body)` | `jwtVerifySignupCode` + compare | `verify('signup', token, code)`, then check `data.email === email` (lower-cased) |
| `sendChangeEmailCode(newEmail, password)` | `jwtService.signAsync({ email, newEmail, code })` | `issue('change-email', { userId, email, newEmail }, baseConfig.emailCodeExpiresIn)` |
| `changeEmail(email, token, code)` | `jwtService.verifyAsync` + compare | `verify('change-email', token, code)`, then check `data.userId === cls user.id` and `data.newEmail === email.toLowerCase()` |

Clean-up: delete `jwtSignupCode` and `jwtVerifySignupCode`. Remove `JwtService` from `LocalAuthService` if nothing else uses it (today nothing else does).

**Deploy note:** a JWT token issued before the deploy stops working afterwards. Those users click "Get code" again. Codes expire within 30 minutes anyway.

